Subject: Borrow frame receivers for element loads

What this does
  Removes a local or argument receiver load when a bounded straight-line window
  computes an element key. The element load reads the frame slot directly while
  the frame remains its owner.

Why we need it
  Computed element reads otherwise duplicate the receiver, push and pop a stack
  slot, dispatch its load, and release it immediately.

What it adds
  A scan over the pre-resolution instruction stream admits a window whose
  simulated operand depth reaches the consumer without crossing a label, a
  branch, a call, a receiver write, or a slot that closures, eval, with or a
  referenced arguments object can reach. The rewrite deletes the receiver's push
  and emits a borrowed-receiver element load carrying the frame slot instead; the
  receiver is never placed on the operand stack, and the slow property path takes
  a real reference for the duration of any user code it can run. An armed rewrite
  that is not consumed fails compilation rather than shipping a short stream.

Needs a bytecode version bump
  Yes. The final series-wide bump remains in 9999.
